# Expectation record for 10_declarations_and_types/008_noreturn.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 008_noreturn
area               = 10_declarations_and_types
description        = _Noreturn function declaration and its effect on reachable code
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The _Noreturn function really never returns: it terminates the program by calling exit, so
control never falls off its end, which would be undefined behaviour.  Every value printed
before that call is fully initialized, and stdout is flushed by normal termination through
exit, so no output is lost.  No header is included and printf is declared by hand, so nothing here depends on a
standard-library header bcc does not ship.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
before=1
reachable=2
noreturn_reached=1 code=0
END
